Z||000000||Haze_Performance||07-16-2000||10:19 PM||||I have a buddy that offered me his newly built(complete)396cid out of his 1971 Camaro.(It started out as a 350cid) It should be the same install as the 350 right?? I ask because it has a very tall high rise intake w/dual quad's. Ill have to cut up the deck lid. Anyone ever see a install of a engine coming out of the deck? (of a Fiero)Im worried about poor air flow. <P>& yes a small block can be built all to hell. Take a look this was a 351cid at first.  <A HREF="http://www.dssracing.com/396long.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dssracing.com/396long.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Haze_sig_1.gif">   My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>H.M.P. </A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 07-20-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000001||stimpy||07-16-2000||10:34 PM||||Correct me if I'm wrong, folks, but isn't the 396 a big block?<P>As far as putting one in a fiero, I don't have either the guts or wallet stuffings to do it, but I'd love to see it happen.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||07-16-2000||10:37 PM||||A 396 IS a big block. Unless he's got a bizarre bored and stroked small block.<BR>You can tell by the king kong size valve covers.<BR>I think you would have to get a Zumalt type kit to move the whole engine and transaxle over as the Rat motor is about 4" longer than the small block.<BR>I would be interested in hearing somebody elses take on this!<BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Haze_Performance||07-16-2000||10:40 PM||||It started life out as a 350.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Haze_sig_1.gif">  My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>H.M.P. </A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||07-17-2000||12:55 AM||||If you could fit that monstrous 455 into a fiero - Reference High Perf Pontiac from a few months back I woould think It could be done ! especially since it started a 350||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||07-17-2000||02:27 AM||||  V8Archie did a tunnel ram/dual 4 bbl equipped 454 into a Fiero(transversley).  So, yeah, anything is possible.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||07-17-2000||06:31 AM||||I don't question that it is a stroked and bored 350 as much as it being a 396 now. I have never heard of that. I suppose anything is possible. Could it be a 383 maybe? <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||07-17-2000||07:45 AM||||If the engine is a small block Chevy that began as a 350, then it'll fit the same as any small block, using the conversion kit of course. I'm curious as to what bore and stroke was used to reach 396 cubes (which is also a popular early big block engine). I've heard of turning down the main bearing journals of 400 cranks in order to stroke the 350, but that still only gives 383 with 0.030" overbore or 388 bored out 0.060".||||reg||1
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||07-17-2000||11:21 AM||||I'm still waiting for someone to get a pontiac engine into our little beasties. The pontiac 350-455 are all the same block, only differences are the heads, crank, and bore (yes, a 350 pontiac can be bored and stroked to a 455, but it's cheeper to buy one) The great thing about pontiac engines is their streetlight racing ability, being known for their torque. With a pimped 455 there's only 2 engines you should have any fears of, the Hemi, and the Buick 450 (err something arround there) The hemi just because it's an engineering master piece, and the buick just because it's the only small block I've ever heard of that out torques a 455 while they're both stock.<BR>something about having 1 foot pound of torque for every 4lbs of car just seems like such a good idea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> heheheh||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||07-17-2000||12:32 PM||||Buick's GS, Olds's 442, and the Goats all had their own different 455 inch engines in 1970, and they all kicked but.  I believe the Olds 455 put out over 500 ft-lbs of torque in 1970.  I got to drive a 1970 442 once back in high school, when muscle cars were the norm (now I'm showing my age).  <P>Awesome. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||07-17-2000||04:50 PM||||Hemis need a lot of air to make power. If you're not revving the snot out of the motor,  it isn't that great. 421 Pontiacs kicked the crap out of them. Unless you're on the high banks of Daytona they're not that big of deal. I'd rather have a Max wedge over a Hemi  anyday (Then again, I'd take the hemi, sell it, and buy a 421 because I've always wanted one.) As for pontiac motors. A 350 cannot be overbored large enough to put 455 pistons in, so you cannot make a 455 out of a 350.(or 389 or 400). <P>I should put a 301 turbo in a Fiero and see if Car Craft will change their opinions on either of them.<P>I know of a 427 small block (bored and stroked 400) in a fiero that will pull wheelstands  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Seen it in Lima, OH.||||reg||1
Z||000011||hugh||07-18-2000||08:02 PM||||I have a 345hp Chevy crate engine in my 88GT,It's not mounted transversly which causes you to lose the trunk.(no great loss)<BR>There appears to be enough room to use a bigger engine like the big block Olds.The olds big block is the smallest of the big blocks,and is a high torque engine,and there is a lot of speed equipment available for it.It also comes with a transmission that will handle the torque.One more thing,Imagine a 500 cubic inch engine from an Eldorado.HMMMM <p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 07-18-2000).]||||reg||1
